WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.260
A credible offensive-security portfolio should be organized around the roles you want, not around every artifact you have ever produced.

2
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:16.840
A penetration tester, application security engineer, vulnerability researcher, detection engineer, and security consultant may all need different proof.

3
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:21.180
Start by listing the capabilities a reviewer for your target role would care about.

4
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.960
Then choose artifacts that demonstrate those capabilities with enough context to be independently assessed.

5
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.640
A useful portfolio page can be simple.

6
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:38.620
Present a short professional summary, a small evidence matrix, selected write-ups, repositories, disclosures, talks, and contact information.

7
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.000
For each artifact, explain the claim it supports.

8
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:51.860
For example, a repository might demonstrate secure tooling, a write-up might demonstrate vulnerability analysis, and an advisory might demonstrate coordinated disclosure.

9
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.480
Do not make reviewers infer why an artifact matters.

10
00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.040
Private or confidential work can still be represented responsibly.

11
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:11.140
You can describe the type of work, the environment category, the vulnerability class, the business outcome, and the constraints without naming the organization or exposing restricted details.

12
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:16.440
Use ranges, generalized descriptions, and approved references where appropriate.

13
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:23.660
If a client, employer, or program did not authorize publication, do not treat secrecy as a puzzle to work around.

14
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.740
Reputation is built through repeated signals.

15
00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:36.040
Clear communication, respectful disclosure, useful open-source contributions, accurate technical writing, and careful corrections all matter.

16
00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:41.980
So does deleting or rewriting old content that is misleading, unsafe, or exaggerated.

17
00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:48.400
A mature portfolio can include learning history, but it should not preserve careless claims just because they were once public.

18
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.160
The best portfolio creates a trustworthy path for a reviewer.

19
00:01:53.260 --> 00:02:00.620
It shows what you can do, how you know, how others can verify it, what constraints applied, and where your claims end.

20
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.960
That is more persuasive than a wall of badges or dramatic language.

21
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:12.980
Offensive-security reputation grows when your public evidence consistently shows technical depth, ethical judgment, and professional reliability.

22
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.540
Think of the portfolio as a guided review experience.

23
00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:23.120
Lead with the few artifacts that best match the audience, then provide deeper material for people who want to inspect details.

24
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:40.260
The organization itself becomes evidence: it shows that you can prioritize, communicate risk, protect confidentiality, and make complex technical work understandable without forcing reviewers to hunt for the point or guess what each artifact proves under time pressure during review.

25
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.280
That clarity is valuable.
